00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.000 align:start position:0%
 
one of President Obama's closest  advisors is pulling back the curtain on  u.s. foreign policy during the Obama  administration Ben Rhodes was a  speechwriter during mr. Obama's 2008  presidential campaign after the election  he became deputy national security  adviser to the president he was closely  involved in key decisions on Syria Iran  and Cuba in his new book the world as it  is a memoir of the Obama White House  Rhodes takes us behind the scenes of the  biggest moments during the Obama  presidency Ben Rhodes is the brother of  CBS News president David Rhodes welcome  abend hey John let's start with North  Korea before we get to the book the  meeting is back on on the 12th I know  you're not a fan of president Trump's  but doesn't he deserve his due here and  getting this to where it's gotten so far  look I think diplomacy is preferable to  military conflict the question is what  is that diplomacy produce what I think  is most likely as you'll see the North  Koreans make commitments  denuclearize in the future but in the  past we've seen them make those  commitments did not follow through and  the follow through is a question John  let me turn to the book because starting  with with President Trump there's a line  that's already gotten a lot of press I  know this is about the entire  administration but let's start on this  comment from former President Obama what  if we were wrong what exactly does that  mean  well you know President Obama wasn't  unlike a lot of Americans after the  election he was trying to figure it out  and we went through different theories  was it the Russian meddling was it the  campaign that the Democrats ran and we  were on that last foreign trip and I was  sitting with him in his presidential  limousine and he'd read a column saying  you know maybe Democrats that pushed too  far in an embrace of kind of  globalization and had gotten away from  you know that sense of identity that  it's important to people part of which  was what you know President Trump in  stoked but I think frankly the reality  is any number of causal factors led to  president Trump and what I answer in the  book is that question which is I think I  don't think we were wrong  well actually what you say in the book  is that you should have seen it because  you said when you distilled it stripped  out the racism and misogyny we'd run  against Hillary eight years ago with the  very same message Trump had use she's  part of a corrupt estab  Bushman that can't be trusted to change  well I woke up the morning after  election night kind of shell-shocked and  and that's when it hit me you know that  that what he went on a change election  and a corrupt establishment well that  was his message the racism and misogyny  was also part of the message and that's  an important aura but I think you know I  remember election night sitting there in  my friend's apartment Cody Keenan who's  a speechwriter for President mama - and  president Alma called us to talk about  what he was going to say the next day  and we're all just absorbing this news  in this new reality and I do realize in  that moment that I don't think that we  had inhabited the the likelihood of  Trump winning that we you know we hadn't  put ourselves in that place and you had  that shell shock and then the next  morning you start to analyze it and  that's what I'm talking about there in  the book this line has been focused on  by critics of the president's President  President Obama because they say it  repeats a flaw that they've seen in him  throughout his public life which is that  what he meant in that question what if  we were wrong was not about his own  misperceptions but about the electorate  that the electorate was just too slow to  catch his kind of change and that that's  the that's the criticism people saying  then what's your response to them oh  absolutely not John I mean you know what  President mama said his entire political  career is that his bet was always on the  American people  he couldn't have become the first  African American president a two-term  president without the competence of the  American people you know I think what  he's getting at is a bigger phenomenon  in the world again we were on a trip  that led up to that comment that he made  where we saw Angela Merkel in Germany  who had taken a huge flack for letting  in Syrian refugees and literally had a  tear in her eye the last time she saw  President Obama we left Justin Trudeau  sitting there alone wondering what was  gonna happen with President Trump and  now we see we'd seen President Xi  Jinping of China who made common ominous  comment to us that if an immature leader  throws the world in the cast the world  will know who to blame and he was not  talking about President Obama and I  think what he's referring to there is a  sense of globalization of immigration  and trade that we're part of an  enterprise basically a liberal  international order that had pushed very  far at changing the the nature of our  world over the last several decades and  that that the question is did that get  too far ahead of voters in places like  the United States in the United Kingdom  you know who were feeling a sense of  uncertainty because of that change this  book was so personal to me it seemed to  me about you Ben I mean you really  talked about your place in the  administration questioning your what you  were doing there when do you fit in and  being a speechwriter for some of the  most iconic speeches how do you write  for someone that you really didn't know  very well and how you navigated those  waters yeah I mean what I really wanted  to try to convey to readers I was 29  years old when I worked for Brock Obama  I didn't have a plan to be the deputy  national security adviser I wanted to  help them get elected president and I  remember you know you get this great  opportunity to write for this guy it was  a great order but you know I didn't  really know him and so what I did is I  spent you know weeks listening to all of  his interviews trying to get inside his  voice he wrote a very revelatory book  dreams to my father that I must have  read several times and I could lift from  that and put you know passages and  speeches but ultimately you know you  have to forge a connection with the guy  who you're working for and I had some  good opportunities on that campaign to  get close to him you said we didn't  always get things right but the larger  project of believing that America is a  better place when you look at the Trump  administration many people believe that  they're trying to unravel and undo  everything that the Obama administration  did does he feel that way and do members  of the administration feel that  everything that you did the work that  you accomplished that they're now trying  to take it all apart do you feel that  well certainly there are certain  policies that they've targeted you know  Paris climate agreement Cuba TPP there's  not an ideological rationale for why you  would take down all those policies other  than that Barack Obama did them but  again it comes back to the what if we  were wrong question  the you know look I say look we didn't  get everything right and I show I say  that there are things I still don't know  I'm not certain about I'll be think  about the rest of my life I'm sure I'll  be thinking about decision making on  Syria the rest of my life but the fact  that America is a progressive inclusive  country that has a future that is more  inclusive and more diverse that I truly  do believe in frankly I think that  project of the Obama presidency that  will endure for decades that's the  direction America is headed and that's  not the direction that Donald Trump is  headed so I think when history has its  accounting here  America is going to end up looking a lot  more like the Obama presidency than the  trump presidency all right Ben Rhodes  congratulations on the book the world as  it is goes on sale tomorrow

